 and {Lorig-Roach}, Ryan and Loucks, Hailey and Loveland, Jane E. and Lu, Jianguo and Ma, Walfred and Marsico, Franco L. and Medico, Jack A. and Mokrab, Younes and Moosa, Shabir and {Moreno-Ochando}, Avelina and Morishita, Shinichi and Mudge, Jonathan M. and Mwaniki, Njagi and Nassir, Nasna and Natali, Chiara and Negi, Shloka and Ni, Lingbin and Okamoto, Faith and Owa, Chie and Paez, Sadye and Peano, Clelia and Pickett, Brandon D. and Pignata, Laura and Prodanov, Timofey and Radhakrishnan, Anandi and Raney, Brian J. and Raveane, Alessandro and Rechtsteiner, Andreas and Ren, Luyao and Rhie, Arang and Ryabov, Fedor and Sacco, Samuel and Salehi, Farnaz and Sehgal, Aarushi and Shabani, Mahsa and Shahatit, Shadi and Shivakumar, Vikram S. and Sinha, Swati and Smeds, Linnea and Solar, Steven J. and Sollitto, Marco and Soranzo, Nicole and Suner, Marie-Marthe and Suzuki, Yoshihiko and Soylev, Arda and Tierney, Jack A.S. and Tomlinson, Chad and Tricomi, Francesca Floriana and Ungaro, Matteo Tommaso and Varki, Rahul and Walenz, Brian P. and Wang, Charles and Wang, Lisa E. and Wenger, Aaron M. and Whelan, Conor V. and Xin, Zilan and Xu, Zheng and Zhang, Wenjin and Zhou, Ying and Zunino, Giulia and Altemose, Nicolas and Barthel, Floris P. and Boucher, Christina and Bourque, Guillaume and Carroll, Andrew and Cechova, Monika and Chaisson, Mark J.P. and Cheng, Haoyu and {Cook-Deegan}, Robert and Doerr, Daniel and Durbin, Richard and {Fiston-Lavier}, Anna-Sophie and Formenti, Giulio and Fullerton, Stephanie M. and Fulton, Robert S. and Garg, Shilpa and Garrison, Nanibaa' A. and Green, Richard E. and Greider, Carol W. and Gymrek, Melissa and Haeussler, Maximilian and Hashmi, Mohammad Amiruddin and Haussler, David and Ioannidis, Alexander G. and Koren, Sergey and Langley, Charles H. and Langmead, Ben and Lawson, Heather A. and Logsdon, Glennis A. and Makova, Kateryna D. and Martin, Fergal J. and Mitchell, Matthew W. and Ossorio, Pilar N. and Pisanti, Nadia and Prins, Pjotr and Rautiainen, Mikko and Schatz, Michael C. and Scheinfeldt, Laura B. and Shafin, Kishwar and Siren, Jouni and Stergachis, Andrew B. and Abou Tayoun, Ahmad and Uddin, Mohammed and Villani, Flavia and Vollger, Mitchell R. and Ye, Kai and Eichler, Evan E. and Garrison, Erik and Hall, Ira M. and Jarvis, Erich D. and Kenny, Eimear E. and Li, Heng and LoTempio, Jonathan and Marschall, Tobias and Miga, Karen H. and Phillippy, Adam M. and Wang, Ting and Paten, Benedict},
  year = 2026,
  month = jul,
  publisher = {Genomics},
  doi = {10.64898/2026.07.21.739710},
  urldate = {2026-07-26},
  abstract = {A pangenome reference overcomes the inherent limitation of any individual reference genome by integrating the variation present in a population. We present the Human Pangenome Reference Consortium's (HPRC) Release 2 (HPRC2), an openly available, second phase pangenome that is an approximately fivefold expansion in genome number over HPRC Release 1 (HPRC1) and measurable improvement in genome completeness, contiguity, and accuracy. Selecting samples with a principled algorithm prioritising common variant coverage, HPRC2 contributes 460 haplotypes that together capture over 99\% of common variation observed in the All of Us Research Program v8 cohort. Combining high-coverage long and ultra-long reads with modern assemblers and polishers, we produce thousands of telomere-to-telomere (T2T) chromosomes, and relative to HPRC1 halve the number of structurally unreliable regions as well as individual base errors per haplotype. We complement the assemblies with whole genome multiple alignments and gene annotations, and derive formal pangenome coordinate systems for addressing off-reference variation, demonstrating that individual human genomes contain more than one hundred thousand variants not succinctly described with respect to existing reference genomes. We also present the first matched long-read backed pantranscriptome and panepigenome at this scale, provide continuous local-ancestry estimates spanning every genome, and outline a host of new tools and applications that leverage the pangenome resource for improved genomics analysis.},
  archiveprefix = {Genomics},
  copyright = {http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {data,genome-graph,hprc,mypublications,pangenome},
  file = {Lucas/Lucas et al.-2026-hprc2_a_human_pangenome_reference_with_near-complete_coverage_of_common_genetic_variation.pdf}
}

@article{Lunyak2007,
  title = {Developmentally Regulated Activation of a {{SINE B2}} Repeat as a Domain Boundary in Organogenesis},
  author = {Lunyak, Victoria V. and Prefontaine, Gratien G. and N{\'u}{\~n}ez, Esperanza and Cramer, Thorsten and Ju, Bong Gun and Ohgi, Kenneth A. and Hutt, Kasey and Roy, Rosa and {Gar{\'c}ia-{\'D}iaz}, Angel and Zhu, Xiaoyan and Yung, Yun and Montoliu, Ll{\'u}is and Glass, Christopher K. and Rosenfeld, Michael G.},
