nd Hollegaard, Mads and Hougaard, David and Huang, Hailiang and Ikeda, Masashi and Joa, Inge and Kahler, Anna and Kahn, Rene and Kalaydjieva, Luba and Karjalainen, Juha and Kavanagh, David and Keller, Matthew and Kelly, Brian and Kennedy, James and Kim, Yunjung and Knowles, James and Konte, Bettina and Laurent, Claudine and Lee, Phil and Lee, Sang and Legge, Sophie and Lerer, Bernard and Levy, Deborah and Liang, Kung-Yee and Lieberman, Jeffrey and Lonnqvist, Jouko and Loughland, Carmel and Magnusson, Patrik and Maher, Brion and Maier, Wolfgang and Mallet, Jacques and Mattheisen, Manuel and Mattingsdal, Morten and McCarley, Robert and McDonald, Colm and McIntosh, Andrew and Meier, Sandra and Meijer, Carin and Melle, Ingrid and {Mesholam-Gately}, Raquelle and Metspalu, Andres and Michie, Patricia and Milani, Lili and Milanova, Vihra and Mokrab, Younes and Morris, Derek and Mors, Ole and {Muller-Myhsok}, Bertram and Murphy, Kieran and Murray, Robin and {Myin-Germeys}, Inez and Nenadic, Igor and Nertney, Deborah and Nestadt, Gerald and Nicodemus, Kristin and Nisenbaum, Laura and Nordin, Annelie and O'Callaghan, Eadbhard and O'Dushlaine, Colm and Oh, Sang-Yun and Olincy, Ann and Olsen, Line and O'Neill, F and Os, Jim and Pantelis, Christos and Papadimitriou, George and Parkhomenko, Elena and Pato, Michele and Paunio, Tiina and Perkins, Diana and Pers, Tune and PIetilainen, Olli and Pimm, Jonathan and Pocklington, Andrew and Powell, John and Price, Alkes and Pulver, Ann and Purcell, Shaun and Quested, Digby and Rasmussen, Henrik and Reichenberg, Abraham and Reimers, Mark and Richards, Alexander and Roffman, Joshua and Roussos, Panos and Ruderfer, Douglas and Salomaa, Veikko and Sanders, Alan and Savitz, Adam and Schall, Ulrich and Schulze, Thomas and Schwab, Sibylle and Scolnick, Edward and Scott, Rodney and Seidman, Larry and Shi, Jianxin and Silverman, Jeremy and Smoller, Jordan and Soderman, Erik and Spencer, Chris and Stahl, Eli and Strengman, Eric and Strohmaier, Jana and Stroup, T Scott and Suvisaari, Jaana and Svrakic, Dragan and Szatkiewicz, Jin and Thirumalai, Srinivas and Tooney, Paul and Veijola, Juha and Visscher, Peter and Waddington, John and Walsh, Dermot and Webb, Bradley and Weiser, Mark and Wildenauer, Dieter and Williams, Nigel and Williams, Stephanie and Witt, Stephanie and Wolen, Aaron and Wormley, Brandon and Wray, Naomi and Wu, Jing and Zai, Clement and Adolfsson, Rolf and Andreassen, Ole and Blackwood, Douglas and Borglum, Anders and Bramon, Elvira and Buxbaum, Joseph and Cichon, Sven and Collier, David and Corvin, Aiden and Daly, Mark and Darvasi, Ariel and Domenici, Enrico and Esko, Tonu and Gejman, Pablo and Gill, Michael and Gurling, Hugh and Hultman, Christina and Iwata, Nakao and Jablensky, Assen and Jonsson, Erik and Kendler, Kenneth and Kirov, George and Knight, Jo and Levinson, Douglas and Li, Qingqin and McCarroll, Steven and McQuillin, Andrew and Moran, Jennifer and Mortensen, Preben and Mowry, Bryan and Nothen, Markus and Ophoff, Roel and Owen, Michael and Palotie, Aarno and Pato, Carlos and Petryshen, Tracey and Posthuma, Danielle and Rietschel, Marcella and Riley, Brien and Rujescu, Dan and Sklar, Pamela and St Clair, David and Walters, James and Werge, Thomas and Sullivan, Patrick and O'Donovan, Michael and Scherer, Stephen and Neale, Benjamin and Sebat, Jonathan},
  year = 2016,
  journal = {bioRxiv},
  pages = {040493},
  issn = {1061-4036},
  doi = {10.1101/040493},
  abstract = {Genomic copy number variants (CNVs) have been strongly implicated in the etiology of schizophrenia (SCZ). However, apart from a small number of risk variants, elucidation of the CNV contribution to risk has been difficult due to the rarity of risk alleles, all occurring in less than 1\% of cases. We sought to address this obstacle through a collaborative effort in which we applied a centralized analysis pipeline to a SCZ cohort of 21,094 cases and 20,227 controls. We observed a global enrichment of CNV burden in cases (OR=1.11, P=5.7e-15), which persisted after excluding loci implicated in previous studies (OR=1.07, P=1.7e-6). CNV burden is also enriched for genes associated with synaptic function (OR = 1.68, P = 2.8e-11) and neurobehavioral phenotypes in mouse (OR = 1.18, P= 7.3e-5). We identified genome-wide significant support for eight loci, including 1q21.1, 2p16.3 (NRXN1), 3q29, 7q11.2, 15q13.3, distal 16p11.2, proximal 16p11.2 and 22q11.2. We find support at a suggestive level for nine additional candidate susceptibility and protective loci, which consist predominantly of CNVs mediated by non-allelic homologous recombination (NAHR).},
  keywords = {bib-thesis,cnv,disease,neuro,snp-array,sv},
  file = {Marshall et al/marshall_et_al_2016_a_contribution_of_novel_cnvs_to_schizophrenia_from_a_genome-wide_study_of.pdf}
}

