 Krumm, Niklas and Ehmen, Brenna W. and {Data and Research Center} and Schwartz, Sophie and Aster, M. Morgan T. and Cibulskis, Kristian and Haessly, Andrea and Asch, Rebecca and Cremer, Aurora and Degatano, Kylee and Shergill, Akum and Gauthier, Laura D. and Lee, Samuel K. and Hatcher, Aaron and Grant, George B. and Brandt, Genevieve R. and Covarrubias, Miguel and Banks, Eric and Able, Ashley and Green, Ashley E. and Carroll, Robert J. and Zhang, Jennifer and Condon, Henry R. and Wang, Yuanyuan and Dillon, Moira K. and Albach, C. H. and Baalawi, Wail and {All of Us Research Demonstration Project Teams} and Choi, Seung Hoan and Wang, Xin and Rosenthal, Elisabeth A. and {NIH All of Us Research Program Staff} and Ramirez, Andrea H. and Lim, Sokny and Nambiar, Siddhartha and Ozenberger, Bradley and Wise, Anastasia L. and Lunt, Chris and Ginsburg, Geoffrey S. and Denny, Joshua C.},
  year = 2024,
  month = mar,
  journal = {Nature},
  volume = {627},
  number = {8003},
  pages = {340--346},
  issn = {0028-0836, 1476-4687},
  doi = {10.1038/s41586-023-06957-x},
  urldate = {2024-10-12},
  abstract = {Abstract                            Comprehensively mapping the genetic basis of human disease across diverse individuals is a long-standing goal for the field of human genetics               1--4               . The All of Us Research Program is a longitudinal cohort study aiming to enrol a diverse group of at least one million individuals across the USA to accelerate biomedical research and improve human health               5,6               . Here we describe the programme's genomics data release of 245,388 clinical-grade genome sequences. This resource is unique in its diversity as 77\% of participants are from communities that are~historically under-represented in biomedical research and 46\% are individuals from under-represented racial and ethnic minorities. All of Us identified more than~1\,billion genetic variants, including more than~275\,million previously unreported genetic variants, more than~3.9\,million of which had coding consequences. Leveraging linkage between genomic data and the longitudinal electronic health record, we evaluated 3,724 genetic variants associated with 117 diseases and found high replication rates across both participants of European ancestry and participants of African ancestry. Summary-level data are publicly available, and individual-level data can be accessed by researchers through the All of Us Researcher Workbench using a unique data passport model with a median time from initial researcher registration to data access of 29\,hours. We anticipate that this diverse dataset will advance the promise of genomic medicine for all.},
  langid = {english},
  file = {The All of Us Research Program Genomics Investigators et al/the_all_of_us_research_program_genomics_investigators_et_al_2024_genomic_data_in_the_all_of_us_research_program.pdf}
}

