on, Agnar and Jonsson, Hakon and Sulem, Patrick and {GSK} and Sandhuria, Jatin and Richardson, Tom G. and Howe, Laurence and Robins, Chloe and Liu, Dongjing and Albers, Patrick and Pereira, Mariana and Seaton, Daniel and Aulchenko, Yury and Whittaker, John and Dermitzakis, Manolis and Johnson, Toby and Davitte, Jonathan and Ingelsson, Erik and {Johnson \& Johnson} and Molineros, Julio and Zhang, Yanfei and Li, Alexander H. and Baugh, Evan H. and Mlynarski, Elisabeth and Torshizi, Abolfazl Doostparast and {Abdel-Azim}, Gamal and Mautz, Brian and He, Karen Y. and Xi, Jingyue and {Nieves-Rodriguez}, Shirley and Khan, Asif and Xu, Songjun and Liu, Xingjun and Sarver, Brice and Truong, Dongnhu and Temanni, Mohamed-Ramzi and Whelan, Christopher D. and Goretti, Letizia and Khan, Najat and Fraile, Belen and Mansi, Tommaso and Rajagopal, Guna and {Sanger (Velsera Seven Bridges)} and Akhtar, Shaheen and {Austin-Guest}, Siobhan and Barber, Robert and Barrett, Daniel and Bellerby, Tristram and Clarke, Adrian and Clark, Richard and Coppola, Maria and Cornwell, Linda and Crackett, Abby and Dawson, Joseph and Day, Callum and Dove, Alexander and Durham, Jillian and Fairweather, Robert and Ferrero, Marcella and Fenton, Michael and Fordham, Howerd and Fraser, Audrey and Heath, Paul and Heron, Emily and Hornett, Gary and {Hughes-Hallett}, Lena and Jackson, David K. and Jakubowski Smith, Alexander and Laverack, Adam and Law, Katharine and Leonard, Steven R. and Lewis, Kevin and Liddle, Jennifer and Lindsell, Alice and Linsdell, Sally and Lovell, Jamie and Mack, James and Mallalieu, Henry and Mamun, Irfaan and Monteiro, Ana and Morrow, Leanne and Pardubska, Barbora and Popov, Alexandru and Sloper, Lisa and Squares, Jan and Still, Ian and Taylor, Oprah and Taylor, Sam and Tovar Corona, Jaime M. and Trigg, Elliott and Vancollie, Valerie and Voak, Paul and Weldon, Danni and Wells, Alan and Wells, Eloise and Williams, Mia and Wright, Sean and Miletic, Nevena and Lenhardt Ackovic, Lea and {Slavkovic-Ilic}, Marijeta and Lazarevic, Mladen and Aigrain, Louise and Redshaw, Nicholas and Quail, Michael and Shirley, Lesley and Thurston, Scott and Ellis, Peter and Grout, Laura and Smerdon, Natalie and Gray, Emma and Rance, Richard and Langford, Cordelia and {UK Biobank} and Collins, Rory and Effingham, Mark and Allen, Naomi and Sellors, Jonathan and Sheard, Simon and Pancholi, Mahesh and Clark, Caroline and {Burkitt-Gray}, Lucy and Welsh, Samantha and Fry, Daniel and Watson, Rachel and Carson, Lauren and Young, Alan and {Illumina} and Mehio, Rami and {Schulz-Trieglaff}, Ole},
  year = 2025,
  month = aug,
  journal = {Nature},
  issn = {0028-0836, 1476-4687},
  doi = {10.1038/s41586-025-09272-9},
  urldate = {2025-08-11},
  abstract = {Abstract                            Whole-genome sequencing provides an unbiased and complete view of the human genome and enables the discovery of genetic variation without the technical limitations of other genotyping technologies. Here we report on whole-genome sequencing of 490,640 UK Biobank participants, building on previous genotyping effort               1               . This advance deepens our understanding of how genetics associates with disease biology and further enhances the value of this open resource for the study of human biology and health. Coupling this dataset with rich phenotypic data, we surveyed within- and cross-ancestry genomic associations and identified novel genetic and clinical insights. Although most associations with disease traits were primarily observed in individuals of European ancestries, strong or novel signals were also identified in individuals of African and Asian ancestries. With the improved ability to accurately genotype structural variants and exonic variation in both coding and UTR sequences, we strengthened and revealed novel insights relative to whole-exome sequencing               2,3               analyses. This dataset, representing a large collection of whole-genome sequencing~data that is available to the UK Biobank research community, will enable advances of our understanding of the human genome, facilitate the discovery of diagnostics and~therapeutics with higher efficacy and improved safety profile, and enable precision medicine strategies with the potential to improve global health.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {data,population,sequencing,short-reads,ukbiobank},
  file = {The UK Biobank Whole-Genome Sequencing Consortium/The UK Biobank Whole-Genome Sequencing Consortium et al.-2025-whole-genome_sequencing_of_490,640_uk_biobank_participants.pdf}
}

