nd potential involve-ment in complex traits 2--4 . Beyond medical genetics, STRs have applications in population genetics, forensics, and single-cell-lineage analyses. While several STR callers exist 5,6 , they exhibit limited accuracy, difficulties in calling homopolymer runs, sen-sitivity to PCR stutter noise, and limited functionality compared with that of SNP callers. As a result, researchers in large-scale projects 7--9 are reluctant to consider STR variants and essentially ignore many of the most variable parts of the genome. To facilitate the incorporation of STRs into large-scale genetic studies, we developed a novel tool called haplotype inference and phasing for STRs (HipSTR). HipSTR builds on our extensive experience with STR genotyping 5 , addresses major limitations of existing STR tools, and is designed to analyze Illumina sequencing data (Supplementary Fig. 1). Briefly, HipSTR begins by learning a parametric model that captures each STR's stutter noise profile. Using the genomic location of the repeat, it then harnesses},
